= Wave 2 — The Gilded Age

== The Story

#storybox[
  Bells rang over the gilded capital as merchant caravans filed through the gates, and the guild halls filled with talk of new corporations and foreign gold. In the temple quarter, masons raised the last of the great cathedrals, their spires catching the evening light. Beneath the city, however, another trade was being plied — a veiled figure slipped past the watch, a scroll of siege plans tucked inside her cloak, and the spymaster smiled at how cheaply a rival's secrets could be bought.
]

== Game Setup

Step up to *Prince* if you have not already, and aim for *Monarch* once you are comfortable. Play *Continents* or *Fractal* on a *Standard* or *Large* map — both give you room to build a tall heartland and then push outward. Keep *Normal* or *Epic* speed; either suits this stage, and Epic gives your newer mid-game units more time to matter.

== Corporations

Corporations are BTS's answer to a late-game economy: you found one by spending a *Great Person* together with a specific technology, then spread it with *executives* — special units that, like missionaries, carry the corporation into cities. Headquarters cities collect gold from every branch, including foreign ones, and each corporation converts different resources into different yields: some turn grain into food, others turn metals into production.

#tip[
  Read each corporation's Civilopedia entry before you commit a Great Person — the resource mix and yield type decide everything about where it earns.
]

Founding early is generally wise, since a corporation compounds across every city it reaches. If your empire runs on food, favor a food-generating corporation; if you are rich in metals, the industrial ones pay off faster. Either way, expect foreign branches to fund your capital handsomely.

== Espionage

Spies unlock a quiet arsenal of missions: *steal technology*, *poison water*, *revolt city*, *sabotage production*, and more. Every mission costs espionage points, which you accumulate with the *espionage slider* — a share of your commerce turned into points — and through spy specialists. The *weighting* in the espionage screen splits your points between rivals; weight a dangerous neighbor heavily, and both your missions and your passive detection of their plots improve.

#warn[
  Espionage is a slow investment. A stolen tech is wonderful, but the same slider could have bought you the tech in beakers — spend points where the target is far ahead, not on parity rivals.
]

== Religion and Culture

A state religion rewards you with faster construction of temples, monasteries, and cathedrals, and your holy city collects gold from every city that follows the faith. The *cultural victory* path leans on these buildings: three cities pushed to *legendary* status, fed by cathedrals, great artists, and culture-producing specialists.

The religion civics ask for a tradeoff. *Organized Religion* speeds building but demands a state religion; *Theocracy* trains better units but blocks missionaries; *Free Religion* gives +10% science in every city — the classic choice once your cathedrals are built.

== Civics

Civics come in five categories — *Government*, *Legal*, *Labor*, *Economy*, and *Religion* — and each switch costs a turn of anarchy, so bundle several changes into one switch when you can. *Spiritual* leaders skip anarchy entirely and can experiment freely.

On labor, weigh *Slavery* against *Caste System*. Slavery converts citizens into instant production — perfect for rushing a wonder or a defensive army — while Caste System replaces whipping with unlimited specialists, which feeds great people and research. Most games run Slavery through the mid-game and switch to Caste System once the big wonders are done.

== Great People and Golden Ages

Great person farms concentrate specialists — scientists and priests first, merchants and artists later — in a few food-rich cities with the right buildings, and the National Epic doubles their output there. Bank great people until you can spend two at once for a *golden age*: a burst of +100% to production, commerce, and research that also ends your anarchy if you time it with a civic switch.

#info[
  A golden age also refreshes your great person progress — trigger it just after a great person is born, never right before one.
]

== Mid-Game Warfare

Build *counter units*, not just hammers: pikemen stop mounted, macemen and crossbowmen rule the field until gunpowder. Siege is how you take cities — catapults and trebuchets soften defenses and wound stacks, so bring plenty and protect them. Promote *veterans* instead of building fresh units: every survivor with a combat promotion beats a replacement, and upgrading them with gold later preserves their experience.

#tip[
  Withdraw your wounded veterans to heal rather than grinding them down; an experienced core that survives wave after wave becomes unstoppable when you reach cuirassiers and riflemen.
]

